  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 109 PM EDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis/Forecast:\n\nAll eyes currently focused on tropical enhanced moisture plume \nrotating across lower Michigan. Combination of southeast dropping \ncold front and rather intense upper jet dynamics working over this \nmoisture axis, resulting in rather widespread light to moderate rain \nacross a good portion of lower Michigan. Post cold front drying has \nkept eastern Michigan largely dry. Despite the widespread rain and \nclouds, still relatively mild out there...with current readings \nmostly in the 60s.\n\nOverhead cold front will continue to work off to the southeast, \ntaking the rain along with it this evening. Expansive area of high \npressure will follow, building across the Northwoods to kick off the \nweekend. Next northern stream wave set to visit the area later \nSaturday into Sunday, bringing our next chance for some showers. \n\nPrimary Forecast Concerns:\n\nPrimary focus centers on overnight temperature trends Friday night. \nSecondary focus on cloud and shower trends.\n\nDetails: \n\nRain will end from northwest to southeast this evening as the cold \nfront clears the area. High pressure to follow, although will need to \nwatch for a few lake enhanced showers off eastern Lake Superior \nFriday as northern stream wave passes across the big lake. \nConceptually, Friday night is shaping up to be a chilly one as high \npressure centers overhead...bringing clear skies and light/calm \nwinds. Only concern is the wet soil conditions preventing ideal \nradiation cooling conditions. Still, thinking at least widespread \nfrost producing temperatures down into the 30s are likely away from \nthe big waters...with some of our typical ice box locations perhaps \nmaking a run into the upper 20s. Much too early for any specific \nheadlines, but will continue to highlight this concern in our \ngraphics. Just about typical early October weather for this weekend, \nwith near normal temperatures and fast moving relatively moisture \nstarved wave bringing additional shower chances Saturday night into \nSunday. Not a whole lot to talk about heading into next week with a \nnorthern stream flow regime that will be completely detached from \nany tropical moisture connection. Passing waves within that northern \nstream will likely bring some mid to late week shower chances, but \ndefinitely not seeing any widespread rain events at this time. Cool \nsurge of temperatures behind Sunday's departing wave, likely \nproducing another round of sub-freezing temperatures Monday night. \nOtherwise, looking at temperatures that will run well within the \nrange of what is considered normal heading through the week.",
